<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sousse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hadrumetum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sousse is a city on the eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> as Hadrumetum and later the capital of the Roman province of Byzacena. Renamed Justinianopolis under Byzantine rule, it became an Aghlabid naval base after the Arab conquest, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribat</a> and a Great Mosque built in the 9th century. Its medina is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sousse.jpg" alt="Sousse" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mietek Ł · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunezja,_Sousse_-_panoramio_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sousse lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> coast of central-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, on the Gulf of Hammamet, about 140 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. It stands between two wadis, the Wadi Bliban to the north and the Wadi al-Halluf to the south-east. The present city has grown to include the ruins of the ancient Hadrumetum. It has long been an agricultural and commercial centre, and an olive grove covering more than 2,500 square kilometres has been one of its riches since antiquity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tyrians founded Hadrumetum in the 11th century BCE as a trading post on the routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>, at a river mouth about 9.7 km north of old Sousse. It became part of the Carthaginian world and appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>, in the third of which it gained territory and privileges by aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a>. It was the second city of Roman Africa after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and became the capital of Byzacena under the Diocletianic Reforms. Its natives included the jurist Salvius Julianus and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodius-albinus">Clodius Albinus</a>. Extensive Roman and Byzantine catacombs lie beneath the city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> sacked the city in 434, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> retook it in 534 and rebuilt its fortifications and churches. It was sacked again during the Umayyad conquest in the 7th century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> a new town called Susa was established near the ruins and served as their main port, from which the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was launched in 827. Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> was known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> cloth called Sūsī and for its robes. Norman Sicily briefly occupied it in the 12th century, it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the 16th, and a French and Venetian fleet bombarded it in the 18th.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> became a French protectorate in 1881, and railways built between 1896 and 1911 linked Sousse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> it had about 25,000 inhabitants, including around 10,000 French. It suffered 39 bombardments between December 1942 and May 1943. It became a governorate capital after independence in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The medina, with its walls, souks, kasbah and mosques, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, listed in 1988. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribat</a>, a combined watchtower and minaret of the Aghlabid period, was built with marble taken from the churches of Melite, and the Great Mosque at the entrance to the medina dates from the 9th century. The city is the capital of the Sousse Governorate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>'s third-largest city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a>. Tourism and the port are important to its economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sousse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hundreds of towns flourished, from Carthage and <b>Hadrumetum</b> to Thugga, Thuburbo Maius, Sufetula, Bulla Regia, Thysdrus and Leptis Magna, with forums, baths, theatres, amphitheatres and floor mosaics of outstanding quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ribats guarded the coast at <b>Sousse</b> and Monastir, and the Ribat of Sousse and the Ribat of Monastir still stand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The southern part of the old province, the region known as Byzacium, became Byzacena, with its capital at <b>Hadrumetum</b> (Sousse), and was governed by a consularis within the Diocese of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzacena">Byzacena</a></li><li><blockquote>His admiral George of Antioch took Tripoli in 1146, and in 1148 Mahdia, <b>Sousse</b> and Sfax fell as the Zirid ruler al-Hasan fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li></ul></section>
